## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design production-grade QoS for multi-tenant streaming: dynamic scheduling, backpressure, and observability under mixed workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation and latency budgets
- Windowed credits and weighted fair queuing
- Backpressure and spill-to-disk strategies
- Observability: latency percentiles, fairness metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
class TenantPool {
  constructor() {
    this.credits = new Map(); // tenantId -> credits
    this.queues = new Map(); // tenantId -> queue
  }
  tryConsume(tenantId, ms) {
    const c = this.credits.get(tenantId) || 0;
    if (c + ms <= budgetFor(tenantId)) {
      this.credits.set(tenantId, c + ms);
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle misbehaving tenants that ignore budgets?
- How would you adapt the scheduler to dynamic topic priorities and outages?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a real-world KCNA pattern: dynamic routing controlled by a side-input, stateful enrichment with TTL, and delivery guarantees in the presence of config changes.

## Key Concepts

- State stores and TTL caches
- Side-input config streams
- Per-message offsets and at-least-once delivery
- ID-based deduplication across routing versions

## Code Example

```javascript
class RouterSink {
  constructor() {
    this.cache = new TTLCache({ ttl: 5 * 60 * 1000 }); // 5 minutes
  }

  onMessage(ctx, event) {
    const cached = this.cache.get(event.type);
    const route = cached?.route || 'default';

    emit({
      id: event.id,
      type: event.type,
      payload: event.payload,
      route,
      ts: event.ts
    });
  }

  onConfigUpdate(ctx, routingConfig) {
    for (const rule of routingConfig.rules) {
      this.cache.set(rule.eventType, { 
        route: rule.route, 
        version: routingConfig.version 
      });
    }
  }
}
```

